course: dictionary
lesson: 4027
title: One word, two meanings: gauche
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. The word «gauche» means awkward and clumsy in English, but in French it means left. The words around it show you which meaning is right.
explain: In a story, a shy teenager who spills drinks and says the wrong thing at a party is gauche, so the meaning is awkward and clumsy. In a French lesson, if you see «à gauche» or «tournez à gauche», then gauche means left. Always check the words around the word first.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to test it. «English borrowed gauche to mean clumsy and adroit to mean skilful.» Clumsy fits the meaning of gauche in English, and skilful belongs to the word adroit, which is its opposite.
fact: «Gauche» means awkward and clumsy in how you behave with other people.
fact: «Gauche» means left in French, as in «à gauche» (to the left).
fact: «Tournez à gauche» means «turn left».
fact: English borrowed «gauche» from French, and it is pronounced «gohsh».
fact: Getting around a French town needs only a handful of words, such as «tout droit» (straight on), «à gauche» (left) and «à droite» (right).
exercise: At a party, a shy boy gives a gauche bow and knocks the vase over. What does gauche mean here? | *Awkward and clumsy | Loud and cheerful | Graceful and elegant | Lost on the way
exercise: In a French lesson the teacher says «tournez à gauche». What should you do? | Go straight on | Turn right | *Turn left | Stop and sit down
exercise: Which French phrase means left? | tout droit | à droite | *à gauche
test: At a party, Ella keeps spilling her drink and saying the wrong thing. What does gauche tell you about her? | Calm and polite | Very rich | *Awkward and clumsy | Asleep
test: The sign says «à gauche». Which way do you go? | To the right | Backwards | *To the left | Straight on
exam: «English borrowed gauche to mean clumsy and adroit to mean skilful.» Which meaning of gauche is used in English? | Left | Skilful | *Clumsy | Happy
